

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I lay out approaches. It's about wiring, and it matters because it decides whether unrelated
  files get touched.

  [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m as a classic [38;5;153m<script>[39m (globals, no modules), while [38;5;153msrc/[39m is CommonJS for Node. A preferences
  module has to be reachable from the browser [3mand[0m from a test runner, and those two conventions don't currently meet
  anywhere.

  - [1mDual export (CommonJS + browser global)[0m — [38;5;153msrc/preferences.js[39m assigns [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m when it exists, otherwise hangs
    the API off [38;5;153mwindow[39m. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains one [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m; Node tests [38;5;153mrequire()[39m it. Matches the repo
    as-is, touches no existing file's module style. Cost: the dual-export shim is a slightly dated pattern.
  - [1mES modules everywhere[0m — set [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m in [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, convert [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m off [38;5;153mrequire[39m,
    switch [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m. Cleanest end state. Cost: converts two files unrelated to this task,
    which is exactly the kind of drive-by refactor I'd normally avoid.
  - [1mESM for the new file only[0m — [38;5;153msrc/preferences.mjs[39m, browser uses [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m, Node tests import it natively. No
    unrelated files touched and no shim. Cost: the repo ends up with two module conventions side by side, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m
    must also become a module to import it.

  Recommendation: [1mdual export.[0m It's the only one that adds the subsystem without restyling code that has nothing to do
  with preferences, and the shim is three lines confined to the bottom of one new file.
